//! Cooperative in-process channels for the async runtime.
//!
//! This file provides two quite different things that both go by the word
//! "channel", kept together because they solve the same problem at different
//! layers:
//!
//!   1. `AsyncChannel<T>`, a pure-Kyte, typed, cooperatively scheduled channel.
//!      It is the one you normally reach for. It carries values of any type `T`,
//!      buffers them in an ordinary `list.List<T>`, and blocks a coroutine (not
//!      an OS thread) when there is nothing to receive or no room to send.
//!   2. `chanNew` / `chanSend` / `chanRecv` / `chanFree`, thin wrappers over the
//!      C++ runtime's native integer channel (`kyte_chan_*`). These predate the
//!      typed channel and survive as a low-level, untyped `long`/`int` seam.
//!
//! Why a Kyte-level channel at all, when the runtime already has one? Because
//! the web runtime is single-reactor and coroutine-based (see
//! [`concurrency`] direction notes): the units that need to rendezvous are
//! coroutines multiplexed onto one thread, not threads. Blocking such a unit
//! must NOT block the thread, so `AsyncChannel` never calls a locking primitive.
//! It parks the current coroutine instead, by recording its handle in a waiter
//! list and calling `coroSuspend()`, and the counterparty wakes it later with
//! `kyte_sched_schedule()`. A blocking mutex inside an async path is a known
//! severe pessimisation on this runtime, so the whole type is built to avoid it.
//!
//! Design and invariants worth holding in mind:
//!
//!   - A coroutine handle is just a `long` (a frame address). The waiter lists
//!     hold these raw handles. A parked coroutine appears in exactly one send or
//!     receive waiter list at a time for a plain `send`/`recv`; `selectRecv`
//!     deliberately registers ONE handle across many channels at once and
//!     unregisters from all of them on wake, so a handle can briefly sit in
//!     several receive lists together.
//!   - Wakeups are a hand-off, not a broadcast: a completed `send` wakes at most
//!     one waiting receiver, and a completed `recv` wakes at most one waiting
//!     sender. The woken coroutine re-checks its loop condition when it resumes,
//!     so a spurious or racey wake simply parks again rather than proceeding on a
//!     false assumption. This is why both `send` and `recv` loop with `while`
//!     rather than testing the condition once.
//!   - `capacity <= 0` means UNBOUNDED: the `capacity > 0` guard in `send` is
//!     false, so a send never blocks and never records a sender waiter. A
//!     positive `capacity` makes the channel bounded and enables back-pressure.
//!     There is no lower bound check; a negative capacity behaves as unbounded.
//!   - FIFO order in and out: the buffer is used head-to-tail (`push` to append,
//!     `at(0)` + `remove(0)` to take the oldest), and waiters are woken oldest
//!     first, so both values and blocked coroutines are served in arrival order.
//!
//! The type is NOT thread-safe and is not meant to be. It assumes a single
//! reactor thread and cooperative scheduling; there is no lock around the buffer
//! or the waiter lists because two coroutines on one thread never touch them at
//! the same instant. Sharing an `AsyncChannel` across OS threads would corrupt
//! the lists. For cross-thread or cross-reactor signalling, use the runtime's
//! own facilities rather than this type.

import list;

/// A typed, bounded-or-unbounded channel that blocks COROUTINES, not threads.
///
/// Values of type `T` are buffered in insertion order in [`AsyncChannel.buffer`]
/// and delivered oldest first. A receiver on an empty channel and a sender on a
/// full one both park the current coroutine (record its handle, then
/// `coroSuspend()`) instead of spinning or blocking the thread; the
/// counterparty wakes exactly one parked coroutine when it makes progress.
///
/// Construct with a capacity: a positive value bounds the buffer and turns on
/// back-pressure, while zero or a negative value makes the channel unbounded so
/// [`AsyncChannel.send`] never blocks. See the file header for the full
/// concurrency contract; in short, this type is single-reactor only and not
/// safe to share across OS threads.
pub class AsyncChannel<T> {
    /// The in-flight values, held oldest first. `send` appends with `push`;
    /// `recv` takes the head with `at(0)` then `remove(0)`, giving FIFO delivery.
    /// Its `size()` is both the queue depth and, against `capacity`, the
    /// fullness test that drives back-pressure.
    buffer: list.List<T>,
    /// Maximum number of buffered values before a `send` must wait. A positive
    /// value bounds the channel; zero or negative means unbounded, in which case
    /// `send` never parks a sender. Not re-validated after construction.
    capacity: int,
    /// Handles of coroutines parked inside `send` because the buffer was full.
    /// Woken oldest first by `recv` after it frees a slot. Only ever populated
    /// when `capacity > 0`, since an unbounded channel never blocks a sender.
    sendWaiters: list.List<long>,
    /// Handles of coroutines parked inside `recv` (or registered by
    /// [`selectRecv`]) because the buffer was empty. Woken oldest first by `send`
    /// once a value is available, and pruned by [`AsyncChannel.unregisterRecv`].
    recvWaiters: list.List<long>,

    /// Creates a channel with the given buffer capacity and empty waiter lists.
    ///
    /// A positive `capacity` bounds the buffer and enables back-pressure; zero
    /// or a negative value yields an unbounded channel whose `send` never blocks.
    init(capacity: int) {
        self.buffer = list.List<T>();
        self.capacity = capacity;
        self.sendWaiters = list.List<long>();
        self.recvWaiters = list.List<long>();
    }

    /// Sends `val`, parking the current coroutine while the channel is full.
    ///
    /// On a bounded channel (`capacity > 0`) this loops as long as the buffer is
    /// at or over capacity: each turn it records the current coroutine in
    /// [`AsyncChannel.sendWaiters`] and calls `coroSuspend()`, so the thread is
    /// free to run other work. It re-checks fullness on every resume because the
    /// wake is only a hint that a slot MAY have opened, not a guarantee. An
    /// unbounded channel skips the loop entirely and never blocks.
    ///
    /// Once there is room, it appends the value and, if a receiver is parked,
    /// hands off to the oldest one by removing it from [`AsyncChannel.recvWaiters`]
    /// and scheduling it via `kyte_sched_schedule`. At most one receiver is woken
    /// per send. Pairs with [`AsyncChannel.recv`].
    pub async fn send(self: AsyncChannel<T>, val: T): void {
        while (self.capacity > 0 && self.buffer.size() >= self.capacity) {
            self.sendWaiters.push(currentCoro());
            coroSuspend();
        }
        self.buffer.push(val);
        if (self.recvWaiters.size() > 0) {
            let h = self.recvWaiters.at(0);
            self.recvWaiters.remove(0);
            kyte_sched_schedule(h);
        }
    }

    /// Receives the oldest buffered value, parking the coroutine while empty.
    ///
    /// It loops while the buffer is empty: each turn it records the current
    /// coroutine in [`AsyncChannel.recvWaiters`] and calls `coroSuspend()`,
    /// yielding the thread. On resume it re-tests emptiness rather than assuming
    /// a value arrived, so a spurious wake just parks again.
    ///
    /// Once a value is present it takes the head (`at(0)` then `remove(0)`,
    /// preserving FIFO order) and, if a sender is blocked on a full bounded
    /// channel, wakes the oldest one from [`AsyncChannel.sendWaiters`] via
    /// `kyte_sched_schedule` so it can retry its send. At most one sender is
    /// woken per receive. Pairs with [`AsyncChannel.send`].
    pub async fn recv(self: AsyncChannel<T>): T {
        while (self.buffer.size() == 0) {
            self.recvWaiters.push(currentCoro());
            coroSuspend();
        }
        let v = self.buffer.at(0);
        self.buffer.remove(0);
        if (self.sendWaiters.size() > 0) {
            let h = self.sendWaiters.at(0);
            self.sendWaiters.remove(0);
            kyte_sched_schedule(h);
        }
        return v;
    }

    /// True when a `recv` would return without parking, i.e. the buffer is
    /// non-empty. A non-blocking readiness probe, used by [`selectRecv`] to find
    /// a channel it can drain immediately.
    pub fn ready(self: AsyncChannel<T>): bool { return self.buffer.size() > 0; }
    /// Number of values currently buffered and waiting to be received.
    pub fn depth(self: AsyncChannel<T>): int { return self.buffer.size(); }
    /// Number of coroutines currently parked in `send` waiting for a free slot.
    /// Always zero on an unbounded channel, since such a channel never blocks a
    /// sender.
    pub fn waitingSenders(self: AsyncChannel<T>): int { return self.sendWaiters.size(); }

    /// Adds a coroutine handle to the receive-waiter list without suspending.
    ///
    /// This is the multiplexing hook for [`selectRecv`], which registers one
    /// handle across several channels so any of them can wake it. Callers that
    /// register must later remove the handle (see
    /// [`AsyncChannel.unregisterRecv`]); a plain `recv` manages its own waiter
    /// entry and should not use this.
    pub fn registerRecv(self: AsyncChannel<T>, h: long): void { self.recvWaiters.push(h); }
    /// Removes the first occurrence of handle `h` from the receive-waiter list.
    ///
    /// The counterpart to [`AsyncChannel.registerRecv`]: after a `selectRecv`
    /// wakes on one channel, it calls this on every channel it registered with
    /// so a stale handle is never scheduled again. It scans linearly and stops
    /// at the first match; if `h` is absent it is a no-op.
    pub fn unregisterRecv(self: AsyncChannel<T>, h: long): void {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.recvWaiters.size()) {
            if (self.recvWaiters.at(i) == h) { self.recvWaiters.remove(i); return; }
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
}

/// Waits until any channel in `channels` is receivable and returns its index.
///
/// This is the async equivalent of a `select` over receive operations. Each
/// pass scans the channels in order and returns the index of the first one that
/// is [`AsyncChannel.ready`], giving lower indices priority when several are
/// ready at once. It does NOT consume the value; the caller then calls `recv`
/// on the returned channel to take it.
///
/// If none is ready, it registers the current coroutine's handle on EVERY
/// channel (via [`AsyncChannel.registerRecv`]) so a send on any of them can wake
/// it, suspends, and on resume unregisters that handle from ALL of them (via
/// [`AsyncChannel.unregisterRecv`]) before looping to re-scan. Registering
/// everywhere and cleaning up everywhere is what makes the wait fair across
/// channels and prevents a stale handle lingering in a list it no longer waits
/// on. An empty `channels` list returns -1 rather than parking forever.
pub async fn selectRecv<T>(channels: list.List<AsyncChannel<T>>): int {
    while (true) {
        let i = 0;
        while (i < channels.size()) {
            if (channels.at(i).ready()) { return i; }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (channels.size() == 0) { return -1; }
        let h = currentCoro();
        let j = 0;
        while (j < channels.size()) { channels.at(j).registerRecv(h); j = j + 1; }
        coroSuspend();
        let k = 0;
        while (k < channels.size()) { channels.at(k).unregisterRecv(h); k = k + 1; }
    }
}

/// Creates a native runtime integer channel and returns its opaque handle.
///
/// This is the low-level, untyped seam over the C++ runtime's `kyte_chan_new`,
/// distinct from [`AsyncChannel`]: it carries plain `int` values and is
/// referred to by a raw `long` handle rather than a Kyte object. The `0`
/// argument requests the runtime's default configuration. The handle must
/// eventually be released with [`chanFree`].
pub fn chanNew(): long {
    return kyte_chan_new(0);
}

/// Sends one integer on the native channel identified by handle `ch`.
///
/// A direct pass-through to the runtime's `kyte_chan_send`; blocking and
/// buffering semantics are whatever that native channel implements.
pub fn chanSend(ch: long, val: int): void {
    kyte_chan_send(ch, val);
}

/// Placeholder receive on a native channel: currently always returns 0.
///
/// Unlike its `chanSend` sibling this does NOT call into the runtime and does
/// not actually take a value off the channel; it is an unimplemented stub that
/// yields a constant 0. Use [`AsyncChannel.recv`] for real receiving. Kept so
/// the native-channel API surface stays symmetric.
pub fn chanRecv(ch: long): int {
    return 0;
}

/// Frees the native channel identified by handle `ch`.
///
/// Releases the runtime resources allocated by [`chanNew`] via
/// `kyte_chan_free`. The handle must not be used again after this call.
pub fn chanFree(ch: long): void {
    kyte_chan_free(ch);
}
